NoteLearning objectives



By the end of this lecture you will be able to:

write the simple linear regression model and state its LINE assumptions;
fit the model in R and interpret the intercept and slope in context;
partition variability with ANOVA and interpret the MSE and \(R^2\);
test the slope with a t-test and the overall model with an F-test;
tell a confidence interval for the mean response apart from a prediction interval;
check the assumptions with residual diagnostics and influence measures.

NoteLearning objectives



By the end of this lecture you will be able to:

explain why separate binary models are a poor substitute for one model of a multi-category outcome;
fit a multinomial (polytomous) logistic regression with nnet::multinom() and interpret relative risk ratios;
fit a proportional odds model with MASS::polr() and interpret cumulative odds ratios;
check the proportional odds assumption with cut-point models and the Brant test;
choose between the multinomial and ordinal models using the assumption check and AIC.

NoteLearning objectives



By the end of this lecture you will be able to:

build a maximum model and explain the trade-off between overfitting and underfitting;
compare models with \(R^2\), adjusted \(R^2\), the partial F-test, AIC, and BIC;
run best subsets, backward, forward, and stepwise selection in R and describe their limits;
select covariates for an associative model with the 10% change-in-estimate rule;
estimate out-of-sample prediction error with k-fold cross-validation.

NoteLearning objectives



By the end of this lecture you will be able to:

fit a multiple logistic regression model and interpret adjusted odds ratios, including for rescaled continuous predictors;
explain maximum likelihood estimation and compare Wald and likelihood ratio tests;
test an interaction in a logistic model and report stratum-specific odds ratios;
assess goodness of fit with deviance, McFadden’s pseudo-R², the Hosmer-Lemeshow test, and a calibration plot;
assess discrimination with an ROC curve and the AUC, and check linearity in the logit, influence, and collinearity.

NoteLearning objectives



By the end of this lecture you will be able to:

follow a seven-step workflow from research question to reported model;
screen candidate predictors and build a multivariable logistic model with gtsummary tables;
assess confounding with the 10% change-in-estimate rule and effect modification with a likelihood ratio test;
check model fit, discrimination, and collinearity before reporting;
write a results paragraph whose wording follows the estimates, and avoid common pitfalls.

NoteLearning objectives



By the end of this lecture you will be able to:

tell Type I (sequential) sums of squares from Type III (partial) sums of squares, and explain why only Type I depends on the order of the variables;
carry out a partial F-test for one variable by comparing a full and a reduced model;
show that the t-test for a coefficient and its Type III partial F-test are the same test (\(t^2 = F\));
test a group of variables together with a chunk (multiple partial) F-test;
choose the right test for a question about one variable or a group of variables.

NoteLearning objectives



By the end of this lecture you will be able to:

compute raw, standardized, studentized, and jackknife residuals and say what each is for;
read residual, Q-Q, scale-location, and leverage plots to check the LINE assumptions;
try a transformation of the outcome and judge whether it helped;
find outliers with a Bonferroni-corrected jackknife test, and influential points with leverage, Cook’s distance, DFFITS, and DFBETAS;
measure collinearity with the variance inflation factor and reduce structural collinearity by centering;
recognize the classic diagnostic patterns in simulated examples.

NoteLearning objectives



By the end of this lecture you will be able to:

tell interaction (effect modification) apart from confounding;
assess interaction with stratified models and with interaction terms, and test it with t-tests and partial F-tests;
interpret interactions with two-level, multi-level, and continuous modifiers;
assess confounding with the 10% change-in-estimate rule, one covariate at a time and jointly;
explain why interaction is assessed before confounding and why a mediator is not a confounder.

NoteLearning objectives



By the end of this lecture you will be able to:

explain why a categorical predictor cannot enter a model as numeric codes;
build dummy (indicator) variables for a predictor with two or more categories;
interpret each dummy coefficient as a comparison with the reference group;
change the reference group and explain what changes and what does not;
test a categorical variable as a whole with a partial F-test;
describe effect coding and polynomial contrasts and when to use them.

NoteLearning objectives



By the end of this lecture you will be able to:

explain why linear regression is a poor model for a binary outcome;
move between probabilities, odds, and log-odds, and read the logistic curve;
fit simple logistic regression models with glm() and turn their coefficients into odds ratios;
rescale the odds ratio for a continuous predictor (for example, per 10 years of age);
fit a multiple logistic regression model and compare crude and adjusted odds ratios.

NoteLearning objectives



By the end of this lecture you will be able to:

write the multiple linear regression model and explain what a partial coefficient means;
fit nested models in R and interpret each coefficient, holding the other predictors constant;
test single coefficients with t-tests and the whole model with the overall F-test;
compare models with \(R^2\), adjusted \(R^2\), and the root MSE;
describe the three missing-data mechanisms and what complete-case analysis assumes;
show adjusted predictions, check the residuals, and test an interaction.

NoteLearning objectives



By the end of this lecture you will be able to:

classify a variable as nominal, ordinal, discrete, or continuous, and explain why the type decides the method;
choose and compute measures of center and spread for symmetric and skewed data;
compute probabilities from the normal, binomial, and Poisson distributions in R;
explain the difference between a standard deviation and a standard error;
interpret a 95% confidence interval and the result of a hypothesis test correctly;
run and read one-sample, two-sample, and paired t-tests.

NoteLearning objectives



By the end of this lecture you will be able to:

match the type of outcome (continuous, binary, count, time to event) to a regression model;
explain why logistic regression, not linear regression, is used for a binary outcome;
interpret odds ratios from simple and adjusted logistic models;
describe how adjustment addresses confounding and how dummy variables code a categorical predictor;
test an interaction (effect modification) between two predictors;
check multicollinearity and influence, and compare nested models with AIC, BIC, and likelihood ratio tests.
